WEBVTT

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:02.640
They're brother and sister.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:08.280
Shaun, Emma and Phoebe are on a special assignment.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:14.440
They have left South Africa and travelled 1,000 miles north-west to Namibia.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.200
They're off to meet an old friend, flying vet, Mark Jago.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:27.120
He's a big cat expert with 19 years of African wildlife experience.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:42.960
Mark has asked Emma and Shaun to help him on a very important conservation project - tracking and testing some of the world's wildest lions here in Etosha National Park.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:48.840
Park warden Shayne Kotting is co-ordinating the project.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.480
It's a massive park.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.840
It's 2.2 million hectares.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.720
We've been tracking lions since last week, trying to keep an eye where they are.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.880
We've had very good success on the 19th latitude, here.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.320
We managed to find a few prides.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:07.840
Unfortunately, over the weekend, as Murphy would have it, we've lost all the animals because we've had rain.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.960
So all the prides have dispersed at the moment.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.640
So now we must be very opportunistic.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:17.000
If we spot lions, if we hear about lions, we go and investigate.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.920
If it's a doable pride, we go ahead and do it.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.400
So we're looking at day and night darting?

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.960
Daytime, night time, whenever we can find them.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:32.520
When you think how much we battle, in say 40,000 hectares, to take 1,000 animals off, we're now looking at 30 on 2.2 million hectares.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.280
This is a huge challenge.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.600
A lion in a haystack.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:42.520
Etosha National Park is the third biggest game park in Africa.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.480
It's the size of Wales.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:50.080
To find lions here, Shaun and Mark must first take to the air.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.360
We've got something down there.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:08.080
On your left, on the ground?

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.320
We've got a pride of what looks like four or five below us.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.600
About 20 nautical miles to the west of...

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:27.680
They've spotted a pride, but getting close to them on the ground will require all of Shaun's tracking skills.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.240
Phoebe's come to help.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.040
It's all relatively fresh spoor, but it's all springbok.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.120
No lion.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:45.040
The problem we've got here is, trying to pick up spoor from the ground is basically impossible.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:49.560
The ground has been baked so hard nothing dents it.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.720
Especially the soft pad of a lion.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:56.760
Very difficult to pick up on the grass.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:02.520
This is a very special population of lions - the Etosha lions.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:10.720
Apart from being notoriously aggressive, which worries me slightly, they are also special in the way that they're all what we call relatively clean from diseases.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.520
One of the most important diseases is the FIV virus, which sounds like HIV because it is.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.839
It's the immuno-deficiency virus that is specific for cats.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.600
This is a very special project now to try and test them for the FIV virus.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:43.120
What we're doing at the moment is driving up to all the big thickets where there's lots of shade where we know the lions will lie up during the midday heat.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:58.520
Two nice big males.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.280
Brilliant, we'll get Marcus out.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:00.440
Then we can continue.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.760
Two hours looking and...

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:08.040
With the lions located, Shaun can call in Emma and the rest of the veterinary team.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.560
The Etosha lions rarely come into contact with humans.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:28.480
Their isolation means they are uniquely disease-free.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.480
It also means they're especially dangerous.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.880
With a lot of the reserves we work on with lions, they're very habituated to humans.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:41.240
It makes the capture much easier and they're very used to vehicles driving in.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:45.400
These boys, apparently, aren't that used to vehicles and this is their pad.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.400
We're trespassing.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.400
All right?

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.520
Yes.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.040
Stop...

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:13.640
Stop.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:24.680
Lions are incredibly dangerous and it's one of the species that we won't allow her to come out at all especially as a couple of lions in this area are renowned for being particularly aggressive.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.600
One of the possibilities is that the lions take a dislike to us and jump on the back of the bukkie.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:30.640
An open bukkie.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:36.360
We're crouching down here because the lions are used to a particular silhouette of a vehicle.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:38.000
If we stand up that could really piss them off.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:43.800
We don't want a pissed off lion.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.800
Did I get him?

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.360
Can you see the dart?

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.360
It sounded like a hit.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.920
The dart just dropped a little bit.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.080
We've just got to make sure that's a hit.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.080
We're just letting him move off.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:09.880
The second lion has moved away, but the team must wait for the darted lion to go down.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:15.280
It is frustrating.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:18.520
You want to be out there getting your hands dirty.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:22.480
As long as she doesn't have any pooey nappies we should get away with it.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:29.440
Air conditioner.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:39.880
It's all very well seeing an animal that looks unconscious - it doesn't mean he is unconscious.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:53.000
OK.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:58.400
Do you want some gloves?

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:58.680
Yes, please.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:02.200
Is that a towel for his eyes there?

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.520
That's a towel for his eyes.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:04.680
Thank you.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:05.840
While the animal's down, everybody's dived in to do as many procedures as possible.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.480
While the animal's down, everybody's dived in to do as many procedures as possible.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:15.560
Measurements, blood samples, tick removals, Measurements, blood samples, tick removals, praying mantis removal...

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:15.880
Praying mantis.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.640
He's a young adult.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.280
OK, this is a pulse oximeter.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:26.400
This is a great toy, especially when you're doing this sort of thing in the field.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:31.080
It tells me the pulse rate and also his oxygen saturation as well, which is important.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:35.720
You see the blood tube filling up there nicely now.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:40.039
Just look at those teeth.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.679
You can see the size of these claws.

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:45.080
They could do a huge amount of damage.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:47.960
He's in fantastic condition.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:49.000
He's got lovely muscles.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.560
He obviously works for his food.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:55.840
What I'm trying to do is collect a fresh faecal sample.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:59.600
We're trying to find out if they're carrying parasites, any form of internal parasites.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:07.160
It's not everybody has the opportunity to do such a wonderful thing as put their hand up the back end of a lion.

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:13.200
OK, just giving him the reversal.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:20.920
It can vary according to the individual how long, but in 15-20 minutes he might start lifting up his head and then he'll become more with it.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.560
We should get on the bukkie in case...

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:25.920
It's not 15-20 minutes.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.040
It's five minutes.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:35.920
For the test results to be meaningful, the team must find 30 lions.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:37.520
They need another 29.

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:41.080
But the project has temporarily been put on hold.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:48.600
Emma, Shaun and Mark have had an urgent call from Africat, a cheetah sanctuary.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.440
This morning one of their animals has been found dead.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:57.120
It's the second cheetah to die in six weeks.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:03.120
Dave Houghton, who helps run the sanctuary, needs to find out what's killing his cheetahs.

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:09.600
To solve the mystery, Emma and Mark are going to perform an autopsy on the dead male.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:14.920
This is Moe.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:18.000
As you can smell, it's been a bit of a while.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.640
Are you ready?

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:21.000
Yeah.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:23.280
Got him.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:33.200
Dave, when did you find him?

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:38.080
He was found this morning but I don't actually know how long he's been dead.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:41.400
Maggots already in.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:43.160
But small maggots.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:46.800
This is quite foul.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:51.880
You can see what Mark's able to do now, he can pull the fur without any problems.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:56.880
Obviously, there's a big area of infection around here because if I try and pull the fur up here it's absolutely firm.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.120
This is very sad.

00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:00.760
He's been here for nearly eight years.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:07.120
A lovely success story, hunting for himself, hunting really well.

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.920
Something maybe bigger and better got him.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:13.600
Cheetahs tend to go for plains game.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:19.280
They can have horns and can fight back and that's a possibility - the cheetah could have got injured by the prey.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:26.360
Let's just turn him over and have a look on the other side.

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:30.360
OK, there's another couple of holes here.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:31.640
It's all in the same area.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:36.600
A single oryx horn is not gonna produce that kind of injury.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:37.400
You've got one, two, three, four...

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:41.480
I mean, they are tear wounds as much as puncture wounds.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:44.080
Look at that.

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:50.160
You can see the clean, healthy tissue and there's all the infected wound.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:53.480
It looks like, more than likely, another predator.

00:11:52.520 --> 00:12:00.840
A bigger predator has grabbed hold of him, and carnivores have awful bacteria, same as domestic cats.

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:05.120
I've had most of my damage from domestic cats.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:08.960
They've got rotten bacteria and the infection has gone really deep inside the tissue there.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:12.520
The most likely predator is a leopard.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:18.080
I mean, we've certainly seen the leopard attacking in the past a cheetah when it's been on a kill.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:25.120
Under normal circumstances a cheetah could actually run away, or possibly would run away.

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:31.280
You've got to remembered these are rehabilitated cheetahs, they have not got all their natural instinct.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:45.160
Moe was one of four orphans learning how to fend for themselves in one of Africat's huge rehab enclosures.

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:46.400
Now there are just two left.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:52.040
Ideally, we're best to catch the leopard.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.240
The problem is they're not easy animals to catch.

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:58.040
Box traps take time and to go on foot is difficult and dangerous.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.320
So we're gonna take out the two cheetahs in here.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:08.640
Emma's been doing all the predator darting at the moment so I'm quite chuffed with the opportunity to go and dart.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:17.760
Darting the world's fastest land mammal is a chance for Shaun to show off his marksmanship.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:19.640
But these are not your typical cheetahs.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:24.960
WHISTLES This is Artemis and Apollo.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:28.280
They're brother and sister.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:39.200
I don't think I've ever been this close to something to dart.

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:47.920
Hey, my girl, you're next.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:14:02.160
I could have got a cheetah closer to him so he could have stuck a syringe in but I thought he was close enough.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:04.800
And I nearly missed!

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.800
It's such a unique opportunity to actually dart an animal.

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:12.720
You can call it back so it's not gonna run off and go missing.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:18.680
In fact, I went to find them a couple of months ago when they were looking really thin.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:20.920
I had to go in the bush a bit.

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:26.440
It got so thick I just whistled and eventually they came, but they ran straight past me and they were waiting at the car when I got back to the car.

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:31.040
It's such a pity that we've got to take them out of the camp but it's the safest thing for the cheetah.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:36.920
Have you ever walked with them when they're going out on a hunt?

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:37.040
I haven't, no.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:40.000
They normally go quite quick.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:43.120
That was quite a stupid question, wasn't it?

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:54.160
It was very stupid, but there you go.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:01.360
He's getting wobbly now, he'll go down any second.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:24.680
Teaching any young orphan to survive in the wild is incredibly difficult, especially with cheetahs like Artemis and Apollo.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:28.040
They have come to us when they're young cubs.

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:30.400
They haven't had their mothers' experience.

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:31.680
They're lacking in life skills.

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:34.840
They don't know that when a leopard comes up they must run away.

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:38.920
They're trying to defend their kill when they shouldn't be.

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:48.160
There's a new arrival at the sanctuary - four orphaned warthogs.

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:54.360
Dave's partner Carla has asked Emma to sort out the girls from the boys.

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:58.120
Look at you!

00:15:57.320 --> 00:16:01.120
How on earth did you get these little things?

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:06.080
The people who found them think their mother was attacked by something.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:11.560
They had to, unfortunately, put the mother down, so they had to be hand-reared.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:13.720
And have you got some tusks starting up?

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:15.640
That's the most dangerous bit about you lot.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:18.040
You look cute, but you can be trouble.

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:22.240
Especially if they don't have any fear of humans, which these obviously don't at the moment.

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:24.920
That's the one thing that our guys all run away from, is a warthog!

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:26.800
And they also squeal to high heaven!

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:29.080
And they don't like being picked up!

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:32.720
Are you chewing my boot there?

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:34.560
But they look in really good condition.

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:36.480
We've definitely got a male there.

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:40.640
The males have two warts there.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:42.160
I don't know if you can see on him there.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.080
I need to see your bottom.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:44.120
Little girl there.

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:45.720
And that's another little girl, isn't it?

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:45.920
Yeah, girl.

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:51.680
So he's easy to identify, but how are you gonna tell the difference?

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:55.360
This job requires a feminine touch.

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:57.520
Do you have a colour preference?

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:59.080
That one's probably better, it's a bit lighter.

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:06.680
Which leg?

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:09.119
Well done, little one!

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:10.040
There we go.

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:17.319
It's all right.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:18.640
It's all right.

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:19.680
There we go.

00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:21.599
Little warthog manicure for you as well.

00:17:20.720 --> 00:17:22.640
All done.

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:24.359
That's it, off you go.

00:17:25.119 --> 00:17:28.560
She's even walking like a lady now.

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:31.640
They look like they're walking on high heels.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:32.000
They do, don't they?

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:33.400
They're beautiful little things.

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:39.760
Bye-bye!

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:04.960
While Artemis and Apollo are still under sedation, Mark wants to give them a quick check-up.

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:12.680
Just gonna make sure the rest of them are all right.

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:17.600
These cheetahs may look tame, but they are not pets.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:24.360
If threatened, they'll attack.

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:36.680
Stand by, stand by.

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:41.440
Yeah.

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:43.320
Hold on.

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:47.280
Good drama for you!

00:18:55.760 --> 00:19:02.960
Artemis and Apollo will be looked after in a temporary enclosure until the leopard is caught.

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:08.320
It's a long way from the wild, but at least they'll be safe.

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:11.120
OK!

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:13.440
Enjoy your stay.

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:13.760
Nice little holiday.

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:17.800
Welcome to Okajima Holiday Camp.

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:25.400
In the future, if they're to survive, these orphans must learn when to fight and when to take flight.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:34.800
Two weeks later, their most immediate threat, the rogue leopard, was caught and relocated.

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:38.440
Phoebe, look!

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:43.480
A big chongololo.

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:43.680
Huge!

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:45.120
Wow, Pheebs!

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:48.440
How many legs has it got?

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:50.960
Two...

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:52.080
Six.

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:52.640
Six?

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:52.960
Really?

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:53.800
And they're coming.

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:54.480
Coming!

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:55.120
Hi!

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:56.920
Hi!

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:57.880
Look at all these legs.

00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:58.560
Lots of legs.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:01.200
He's got more than six, hasn't he?

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:05.080
Big congolo.

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:05.360
Big chongololo.

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:06.320
Coming!

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:09.200
Wow!

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:14.480
Look at the legs, Pheebs.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:19.520
Bye-bye, Mr Chongololo.

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:29.160
Back in Etosha, the temperature is rising.

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:32.720
It's over 40 degrees Celsius.

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:37.120
Shaun and Shayne are back out tracking lions.

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:52.160
Finally!

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:54.120
That's a big female.

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:54.440
Yeah.

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:55.800
Looks like a female.

00:20:55.040 --> 00:21:01.080
Pretty much the only soft piece of soil on Etosha.

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:03.400
Lots of activity around here.

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:05.920
Quite a lot of small ones.

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:08.240
A couple of cubs, at least.

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:11.440
There's one set going that way.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:11.760
There's another set over there.

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:15.520
That's at least two cubs.

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:19.680
What's that over there?

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:20.320
Ah!

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:23.080
That looks like it there.

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:30.280
We've managed to find some lions, a female and a couple of cubs.

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:35.480
So what we'll probably do is get the team and come and do these this afternoon.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:51.480
It's too hot for the lions to be darted straight away, so everyone takes the chance to relax until things cool down.

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:58.440
Pretty dresses!

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:02.960
There's lots of down time, and it's very boring to catch up on gossip.

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:05.320
And sometimes you can't be fussy with what you're reading.

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:07.920
But the pictures are great, aren't they?

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:11.720
Gotta just check that you have a full education.

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:16.960
Gotta see the latest in fashion, got to keep up with fashion in the bush(!)

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:17.560
As long as it's khaki.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:21.920
Shaun thinks these sort of magazines are absolutely awful, so I always find him reading them.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:25.160
Did you say "complete waste of money"?

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:29.840
When the heat finally wanes, the team set off again.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:39.760
As we're standing here, how many do they think are in there?

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:42.600
It looks as though there could be as many as ten.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:46.000
They're gonna be a mixture of females and youngsters of various ages.

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:50.880
The females could be four or five, and the rest of them will be juveniles, probably.

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:53.800
I don't think we've seen a male.

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:54.400
Here you are.

00:22:54.040 --> 00:22:56.640
Let's go.

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:07.760
There are five lionesses.

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:20.360
20 metres.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:21.400
Good.

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:21.680
Well done.

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:24.760
A lucky shot, there.

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:27.640
OK, let's just give them two seconds.

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:32.440
No, stop, stop, stop!

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:33.440
Stop!

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:33.960
Stop!

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:35.320
Stop!

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:39.840
She's facing that way.

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:48.600
There's another one somewhere.

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:50.240
There she is.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:52.200
She's just chewing it down.

00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:58.800
They've hit four, but the fifth has gone missing.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:01.480
You're starting to get sleepy.

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:03.880
Where's that other big female?

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:08.680
This is actually quite scary, because we've been told there's five adults here.

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:09.200
We've done four.

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:15.960
We can't spot the fifth one, so we're really reluctant to get off the bukkie until we spot that fifth one.

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:24.200
There are cubs involved here - the most beautiful little cubs - and this usually doesn't affect them, we're processing the mums very quickly.

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:24.920
It's a nice cross-section of this pride here.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:28.160
But this is what the discussion is behind me.

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:30.320
Just keep an eye open.

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:30.560
I don't know where it is.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:31.680
I can't see it.

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:32.320
Get your gun ready.

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:38.160
The last lioness can't be seen, but with night falling, the team must get started.

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:47.960
What we want to do is try and get the animals together, because that's the best way we can control the situation.

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:51.880
I'm checking these anaesthetics - I won't be able to do it if I'm running along.

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:58.560
I don't want to do it if I know there's another female out there who's not been darted.

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:03.800
The cubs are staying close to their sedated mothers.

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:10.360
They don't represent a risk, but the solitary female does.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:14.720
Now the challenge begins.

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:20.920
I'm keeping them anaesthetised long enough for all the procedures to be undertaken.

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:25.400
See the reflex there.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:26.640
A bit of cream.

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:42.400
I'm trying to concentrate, but when you look behind and see the sunset, you realise what a beautiful place Etosha is.

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:44.440
I'm trying to ignore that.

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:48.760
Can we get some light, guys?

00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:54.240
Apart from getting dark, we've got the storms coming in, as well!

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:56.480
So we're really working on a very tight schedule here.

00:25:56.240 --> 00:26:03.760
As the sun sets quickly over Namibia, now is the time predators are most active.

00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:09.440
I just hope someone here is looking out for the fifth female, which is cruising around behind us.

00:26:08.480 --> 00:26:12.200
Because if she comes too close to us, people are gonna start running fairly quickly.

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:19.280
Right, let's start branding them.

00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:20.400
Mark?

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:31.760
The branding will enable the team to match up the test results with each lion.

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:34.200
All he's doing is just burning just the top part of the skin.

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:36.000
He's not going too deep.

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:41.360
As you can see, the animal's not flinching at all, which means I don't need to top her up.

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:43.520
Very nervous now.

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:45.760
The animals have been under for quite a while.

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:46.200
We're just finishing them off.

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:49.360
We wanna make sure they don't wake up.

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:53.640
It's exceedingly dangerous - we have an adult, free-ranging lion.

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:56.440
Guys, look at that storm.

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:57.080
Let's have rain as well.

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:57.520
Nice.

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:05.720
The fifth lioness has been spotted.

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:08.080
She's only 100 yards from the team.

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:14.600
All the darts are out.

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:17.600
I need some muscle power.

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:18.640
Drag.

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:19.480
One, two, three, drag.

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:21.680
OK, that's fine.

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:21.800
No.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:23.800
No.

00:27:22.840 --> 00:27:26.440
No, leave that one, she's waking up.

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:31.000
Everybody ready to move her?

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:36.720
Let's move off a bit, then we can watch them from a distance.

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:39.360
The lioness is getting closer.

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:40.160
It's time for a swift exit.

00:27:50.720 --> 00:27:56.880
See the youngsters coming back just to check on their mums.

00:27:55.920 --> 00:28:01.600
The lionesses are recovering and the cubs are safe.

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:08.680
Five down, 25 to go.

00:28:13.440 --> 00:28:16.440
Next week - the lions fight back.

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:21.160
I've got spoors.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:26.120
Shaun tracks one of the most elusive of wild carnivores.

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:29.560
And he's not very asleep, so just let it down.

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:34.880
And Phoebe celebrates a big day.
